const verifySnippet = `import hmac, hashlib

def verify(payload, header, secret):
    t = int(header.split(',')[0][2:])
    sigs = [p[3:] for p in header.split(',') if p.startswith('v1=')]
    expected = hmac.new(
        secret.encode(), f"{t}.{payload}".encode(),
        hashlib.sha256
    ).hexdigest()
    return any(hmac.compare_digest(expected, s) for s in sigs)`;
---

<Layout {title} {description} canonicalPath="/docs">
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-6 py-16">
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-fg">Docs</h1>
    <p class="mt-4 text-fg-muted">
      Godance is an OpenAI-compatible REST API. If you've shipped against the OpenAI SDK,
      you already know the request shape of every endpoint below, and chat is drop-in: same
      JSON, same streaming format. Image and video generation run as async jobs, and errors
      come back as RFC 7807 problem details; see
      <a href="#openai-compat" class="underline" style="color: var(--color-primary)">OpenAI SDK compatibility</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-12 mb-4 text-fg">Get to first call in three steps</h2>
    <ol class="space-y-3 list-decimal list-inside text-fg-muted">
      <li>
        <a href="https://app.godance.ai/signup" class="underline" style="color: var(--color-primary)">Create an account</a>
        and mint an API key in Settings → API Keys (returned in plaintext once — save it).
      </li>
      <li>Top up with card, ACH, or SEPA (Stripe), or USDT (NOWPayments). Same balance, same API.</li>
      <li>
        Point your OpenAI SDK at
        <code class="code-chip">https://api.godance.ai/v1</code>
        and start switching <code class="code-chip">model</code> values.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <!-- ===== Ask your AI assistant ===== -->
    <h2 id="ai-assistants" class="scroll-mt-24 text-2xl font-bold mt-12 mb-4 text-fg">Ask your AI assistant</h2>
    <p class="text-fg-muted">
      If an AI assistant writes your code, give it one file. It holds every route on this page
      with a curl example and a Python example, using the real field names, so the assistant
      does not have to guess:
    </p>
    <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-fg-muted">
      <li>
        <a href="/llms-full.txt" class="underline" style="color: var(--color-primary)"><code class="code-chip">https://godance.ai/llms-full.txt</code></a>
        — the complete reference. Paste the link, or the file itself, into the conversation and
        say what you want to build.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://api.godance.ai/v1/openapi.yaml" class="underline" style="color: var(--color-primary)"><code class="code-chip">https://api.godance.ai/v1/openapi.yaml</code></a>
        — the same API as an OpenAPI 3.1 description, for tools that read one.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/llms.txt" class="underline" style="color: var(--color-primary)"><code class="code-chip">https://godance.ai/llms.txt</code></a>
        — the short summary.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-4 text-fg-muted">
      Give the assistant an API key through an environment variable
      (<code class="code-chip">GODANCE_API_KEY</code>), not by pasting it into the conversation.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-12 mb-4 text-fg">Endpoint reference</h2>
    <ul class="space-y-2 text-fg-muted">
      {endpoints.map(({ id, label, summary }) => (
        <li><a href={`#${id}`} class="underline" style="color: var(--color-primary)">{label}</a> — {summary}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>

    <!-- ===== Chat completions ===== -->
    <h2 id="chat-completions" class="scroll-mt-24 text-2xl font-bold mt-16 mb-4 text-fg">POST /v1/chat/completions</h2>
    <p class="text-fg-muted">
      OpenAI's chat completions contract. Set <code class="code-chip">"stream": true</code>
      for server-sent events; otherwise the response is a single JSON object.
    </p>
    <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-fg-muted">
      <li><code class="code-chip">model</code> (required) — any text model from the <a href="/models" class="underline" style="color: var(--color-primary)">model catalog</a>.</li>
      <li><code class="code-chip">messages</code> (required) — array of objects with <code class="code-chip">role</code> and <code class="code-chip">content</code>; <code class="code-chip">content</code> is a string, or OpenAI's array of text and image parts (see <a href="#chat-images" class="underline" style="color: var(--color-primary)">images in chat</a>).</li>
      <li><code class="code-chip">max_tokens</code>, <code class="code-chip">temperature</code> — optional.</li>
      <li><code class="code-chip">stream</code> — optional, defaults to <code class="code-chip">false</code>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-4 text-fg-muted">
      Non-streaming returns <code class="code-chip">200</code> with a
      <code class="code-chip">chat.completion</code> object
      (<code class="code-chip">choices[].message</code>, <code class="code-chip">finish_reason</code>,
      <code class="code-chip">usage</code>). Streaming returns
      <code class="code-chip">text/event-stream</code>: one
      <code class="code-chip">chat.completion.chunk</code> per event, then
      <code class="code-chip">data: [DONE]</code>. Other OpenAI parameters
      (<code class="code-chip">tools</code>, <code class="code-chip">top_p</code>,
      <code class="code-chip">response_format</code>, …) aren't supported and are ignored.
    </p>
    <pre class="code-block mt-4"><code>{chatSnippet}</code></pre>

    <h3 id="chat-images" class="scroll-mt-24 text-xl font-semibold mt-10 mb-4 text-fg">Images in chat</h3>
    <p class="text-fg-muted">
      A message's <code class="code-chip">content</code> can be OpenAI's array of parts, so a
      model that can see is sent images the same way OpenAI's SDK sends them. The response is
      unchanged: the answer's <code class="code-chip">content</code> is a string. Streaming
      works the same way.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-fg-muted">
      <strong class="text-fg">Not switched on yet.</strong> Image parts are part of
      <a href="#files" class="underline" style="color: var(--color-primary)">inputs</a>, which are
      being rolled out. Until they are switched on, a request with an image part answers
      <code class="code-chip">403</code> with <code class="code-chip">code: "inputs_disabled"</code>.
      Text parts work now, and a request whose content is a string is unaffected.
    </p>
    <pre class="code-block mt-4"><code>{chatImageSnippet}</code></pre>
    <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-fg-muted">
      <li><strong class="text-fg">Parts.</strong> <code class="code-chip">&#123;"type": "text", "text": "…"&#125;</code> and <code class="code-chip">&#123;"type": "image_url", "image_url": &#123;"url": "…", "detail": "auto"&#125;&#125;</code>. <code class="code-chip">detail</code> is optional: <code class="code-chip">auto</code>, <code class="code-chip">low</code>, <code class="code-chip">high</code> or <code class="code-chip">original</code>, passed on to providers that take it. Images belong in <code class="code-chip">user</code> messages. Any other part type answers <code class="code-chip">400</code> with <code class="code-chip">code: "unsupported_content_part"</code>.</li>
      <li><strong class="text-fg">Three ways to name an image.</strong> <code class="code-chip">url</code> may be:
        a base64 data URL (<code class="code-chip">data:image/png;base64,…</code>);
        an <code class="code-chip">https</code> URL, which Godance fetches once, without following redirects, from public addresses only;
        or <code class="code-chip">https://api.godance.ai/v1/files/&#123;id&#125;/content</code> for one of <a href="#files" class="underline" style="color: var(--color-primary)">your own files</a>, which is read from storage rather than fetched.
        Anything else answers <code class="code-chip">400</code> with <code class="code-chip">code: "invalid_image_url"</code>.</li>
      <li><strong class="text-fg">Limits.</strong> At most 10 images in a request, each at most 20&nbsp;MB, PNG, JPEG or WebP, still images only. A data URL counts towards the request body, which is limited to 256&nbsp;KB (a larger body answers <code class="code-chip">413</code>); for a larger image use an <code class="code-chip">https</code> URL or upload it as a file. An <code class="code-chip">https</code> image must answer within 10 seconds with an image content type.</li>
      <li><strong class="text-fg">Which models.</strong> A model accepts images when <code class="code-chip">godance.capabilities.inputs.images</code> is present on <a href="#models" class="underline" style="color: var(--color-primary)">GET /v1/models</a>; its <code class="code-chip">max</code> is the most images one request to it may carry. The <a href="/models" class="underline" style="color: var(--color-primary)">model catalog</a> lists them. Any other model answers <code class="code-chip">400</code> with <code class="code-chip">code: "model_does_not_accept_images"</code>, and a fallback model that cannot see is never used for a request with images.</li>
      <li><strong class="text-fg">What the provider receives.</strong> The image itself, inline, never your URL. Details stored inside the file, such as where a photo was taken, are removed first. Godance does not keep the image.</li>
      <li><strong class="text-fg">Price.</strong> A provider counts an image as input tokens and reports them in <code class="code-chip">usage.prompt_tokens</code>, which is what you are charged for. The amount reserved up front allows for each image at the most its model can count it as, and the difference is returned when the request settles.</li>
      <li><strong class="text-fg">Refusals,</strong> each before anything is charged: <code class="code-chip">400 image_fetch_failed</code> (unreachable, not a public address, a redirect, not an image, too slow), <code class="code-chip">413 image_too_large</code>, <code class="code-chip">400 image_type_unsupported</code>, <code class="code-chip">400 too_many_images</code>, and <code class="code-chip">404 file_not_found</code> for a file that is not yours.</li>
    </ul>

    <!-- ===== Images ===== -->
    <h2 id="images" class="scroll-mt-24 text-2xl font-bold mt-16 mb-4 text-fg">POST /v1/images/generations</h2>
    <p class="text-fg-muted">
      The request shape is OpenAI's. Every image model runs as an async job, so by default
      this endpoint answers <code class="code-chip">202 Accepted</code> with a
      <code class="code-chip">job_id</code>. Poll
      <a href="#jobs" class="underline" style="color: var(--color-primary)"><code class="code-chip">GET /v1/jobs/&#123;id&#125;</code></a>
      for the result, or pass <code class="code-chip">webhook_url</code> and receive a
      <a href="#webhooks" class="underline" style="color: var(--color-primary)">signed POST</a>
      when the images are ready. To get the images in the response, as OpenAI's API returns
      them, set your API key to
      <a href="#image-responses" class="underline" style="color: var(--color-primary)">synchronous image responses</a>.
    </p>
    <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-fg-muted">
      <li><code class="code-chip">model</code>, <code class="code-chip">prompt</code> (required).</li>
      <li><code class="code-chip">size</code>, <code class="code-chip">n</code>, <code class="code-chip">style</code>, <code class="code-chip">quality</code> — optional, as in OpenAI's API; <code class="code-chip">n</code> defaults to 1.</li>
      <li><code class="code-chip">n</code> can be at most the model's <code class="code-chip">max_n</code> (see <a href="#models" class="underline" style="color: var(--color-primary)">GET /v1/models</a>; 1 when a model doesn't state one). A larger <code class="code-chip">n</code> answers <code class="code-chip">400</code> before anything is reserved.</li>
      <li><code class="code-chip">webhook_url</code> — optional Godance extension; where to POST the completion event.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-4 text-fg-muted">
      The full cost (per-image price × <code class="code-chip">n</code>) is reserved from your
      balance when the job is accepted and refunded automatically if generation fails. A model
      that returns fewer images than <code class="code-chip">n</code> is charged for the images it
      returned, and the rest of the reservation goes back to your balance. An insufficient
      balance returns <code class="code-chip">402</code>.
    </p>
    <pre class="code-block mt-4"><code>{imageSnippet}</code></pre>
    <pre class="code-block mt-4"><code>{acceptedSnippet}</code></pre>

    <h3 id="image-responses" class="scroll-mt-24 text-xl font-semibold mt-10 mb-4 text-fg">Image responses in OpenAI's shape</h3>
    <p class="text-fg-muted">
      Each API key has an <code class="code-chip">image_response</code> setting.
      <code class="code-chip">async</code> is the behaviour above: a job to poll.
      With <code class="code-chip">sync</code>, an image request waits for its job and answers
      with the images, so code written for OpenAI's SDK works with only the base URL and key
      changed:
    </p>
    <pre class="code-block mt-4"><code>{sdkImageSnippet}</code></pre>
    <pre class="code-block mt-4"><code>{syncImageSnippet}</code></pre>
    <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-fg-muted">
      <li><strong class="text-fg">Choosing it.</strong> Pick it for each key on the API keys page of the app, send <code class="code-chip">"image_response": "sync"</code> when you create a key with <code class="code-chip">POST /v1/keys</code>, or change a key with <code class="code-chip">PATCH /v1/keys/&#123;id&#125;</code>. Keys created before this setting existed are <code class="code-chip">async</code> until you change them. <code class="code-chip">GET /v1/keys</code> shows each key's setting.</li>
      <li><strong class="text-fg">The wait.</strong> The request is held for up to about 90 seconds. It is the same job either way: the same price, reserved when the job is accepted, and the same <code class="code-chip">webhook_url</code> delivery. The response adds <code class="code-chip">job_id</code> and <code class="code-chip">cost_cents</code> to OpenAI's <code class="code-chip">created</code> and <code class="code-chip">data</code>.</li>
      <li><strong class="text-fg">Still running at the limit.</strong> The request answers <code class="code-chip">202 Accepted</code> with the job body above and a <code class="code-chip">Retry-After: 5</code> header. The job carries on; read it at <code class="code-chip">poll_url</code>. Code that only reads <code class="code-chip">data</code> should check for the 202.</li>
      <li><strong class="text-fg">Failed.</strong> A job that fails answers <code class="code-chip">502</code> as problem JSON with <code class="code-chip">code: "generation_failed"</code>, the job's error in <code class="code-chip">detail</code> and its <code class="code-chip">job_id</code>. The reserved credit is refunded, as for any failed job.</li>
      <li><strong class="text-fg"><code class="code-chip">response_format</code>.</strong> <code class="code-chip">"url"</code> returns a link per image and <code class="code-chip">"b64_json"</code> returns each image base64-encoded. Without it, GPT Image models return <code class="code-chip">b64_json</code>, as OpenAI's API does, and a <code class="code-chip">url</code> as well; every other model returns a <code class="code-chip">url</code>. A result larger than 25 MB is returned as links only. Links are signed and expire after a while: read the job again for a fresh one.</li>
      <li><strong class="text-fg">One request as a job.</strong> With a sync key, send <code class="code-chip">"async": true</code> in the body or a <code class="code-chip">Prefer: respond-async</code> header to get the 202 at once.</li>
      <li>Video is not affected: <code class="code-chip">POST /v1/videos/generations</code> always answers with a job.</li>
    </ul>
    <pre class="code-block mt-4"><code>{keyResponseSnippet}</code></pre>

    <h3 id="image-edits" class="scroll-mt-24 text-xl font-semibold mt-10 mb-4 text-fg">Edit an image</h3>
    <p class="text-fg-muted">
      <code class="code-chip">POST /v1/images/edits</code> makes an image from images: your product
      in a new setting, a photo in another style, one part of a picture changed. It takes OpenAI's
      own form, so <code class="code-chip">client.images.edit(…)</code> works with only the base
      URL and key changed, and a JSON form that points at images already stored.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-fg-muted">
      <strong class="text-fg">Not switched on yet.</strong> Edits are part of
      <a href="#files" class="underline" style="color: var(--color-primary)">inputs</a>, which are
      being rolled out. Until they are switched on, this route answers
      <code class="code-chip">403</code> with <code class="code-chip">code: "inputs_disabled"</code>.
    </p>
    <pre class="code-block mt-4"><code>{editSdkSnippet}</code></pre>
    <pre class="code-block mt-4"><code>{editCurlSnippet}</code></pre>
    <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-fg-muted">
      <li><strong class="text-fg">The multipart form.</strong> <code class="code-chip">model</code> and <code class="code-chip">prompt</code>, one or more <code class="code-chip">image</code> parts (also accepted as <code class="code-chip">image[]</code>), and optionally <code class="code-chip">mask</code>, <code class="code-chip">n</code>, <code class="code-chip">size</code>, <code class="code-chip">quality</code> and <code class="code-chip">response_format</code>.</li>
      <li><strong class="text-fg">What happens to the images you send.</strong> Each one is checked exactly as an upload to <a href="#files" class="underline" style="color: var(--color-primary)"><code class="code-chip">POST /v1/files</code></a> is, with the same limits and the same refusals, and becomes one of your files. It is kept for as long as the job is.</li>
      <li><strong class="text-fg">The answer</strong> follows the key, as for a generation: a job to poll, or with a key set to <a href="#image-responses" class="underline" style="color: var(--color-primary)"><code class="code-chip">sync</code></a> the images in OpenAI's shape. The SDK example above needs a sync key.</li>
      <li><strong class="text-fg">The same job as a generation.</strong> The price is reserved when the job is accepted and returned in full if it fails; <code class="code-chip">webhook_url</code>, <code class="code-chip">async</code>{recipesOn && <Fragment>, <code class="code-chip">gallery_recipe</code></Fragment>} and <code class="code-chip">recipe</code> work as they do there. The job lists what it worked from under <a href="#job-inputs" class="underline" style="color: var(--color-primary)"><code class="code-chip">inputs</code></a>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-6 text-fg-muted">
      <strong class="text-fg">The JSON form</strong> names images that are already stored, in either
      <a href="#input-references" class="underline" style="color: var(--color-primary)">form of input</a>:
      a <code class="code-chip">file_id</code> for an upload, or a <code class="code-chip">job_output</code>
      for an image one of your own jobs made. Editing a job's output needs no upload at all.
    </p>
    <pre class="code-block mt-4"><code>{editJsonSnippet}</code></pre>
    <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-fg-muted">
      <li><strong class="text-fg">Masks.</strong> A <code class="code-chip">mask</code> says where the first image may change. Send a PNG the same size as that image whose transparent areas are the parts to change. Only a model that lists <code class="code-chip">"mask": true</code> takes one.</li>
      <li><strong class="text-fg">Which models.</strong> A model takes input images when <code class="code-chip">godance.capabilities.inputs.images</code> is present on <a href="#models" class="underline" style="color: var(--color-primary)">GET /v1/models</a>: <code class="code-chip">max</code> is the most images one request may carry, and <code class="code-chip">mask</code> whether it takes a mask. Today: <code class="code-chip">gpt-image-2</code> and <code class="code-chip">gpt-image-1.5</code> (up to 16, with a mask), <code class="code-chip">seedream-5.0</code> (up to 14) and <code class="code-chip">flux-2-pro</code> (up to 8). The <a href="/models" class="underline" style="color: var(--color-primary)">models page</a> lists them all.</li>
      <li><strong class="text-fg">The input price.</strong> Some providers charge for each input image. Where one does, the model's <code class="code-chip">pricing</code> carries <code class="code-chip">per_input_image_microcents</code>, and an edit costs the price of the images made plus that price for each input image, with the platform fee on the sum. A mask is not charged for. To see the figure first, send <code class="code-chip">input_images</code> to <a href="#estimates" class="underline" style="color: var(--color-primary)"><code class="code-chip">POST /v1/estimates</code></a>: it is exactly what the request will reserve.</li>
    </ul>
    <pre class="code-block mt-4"><code>{editCapabilitiesSnippet}</code></pre>
    <pre class="code-block mt-4"><code>{editEstimateSnippet}</code></pre>
    <p class="mt-4 text-fg-muted">
      <strong class="text-fg">Refusals,</strong> each before anything is stored or charged:
      <code class="code-chip">400</code> with <code class="code-chip">code: "input_not_supported"</code>
      when the model takes no input images, takes fewer than were sent, or takes no mask;
      <code class="code-chip">400</code> when there is no image at all; and everything under
      <a href="#input-refusals" class="underline" style="color: var(--color-primary)">what is refused, and why</a>.
    </p>

    <!-- ===== Videos ===== -->
    <h2 id="videos" class="scroll-mt-24 text-2xl font-bold mt-16 mb-4 text-fg">POST /v1/videos/generations</h2>
    <p class="text-fg-muted">
      Always async — video takes minutes, longer than an HTTP request should wait. The response
      is the same <code class="code-chip">202</code> + <code class="code-chip">job_id</code> body
      as for images; poll the job or pass <code class="code-chip">webhook_url</code>.
    </p>
    <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-fg-muted">
      <li><code class="code-chip">model</code>, <code class="code-chip">prompt</code> (required).</li>
      <li><code class="code-chip">size</code> — optional output resolution.</li>
      <li><code class="code-chip">seconds</code> — optional clip length. Left out, the model's standard length is used: 5 seconds for most models, 10 for the Sora models and 6 for <code class="code-chip">minimax-hailuo-2.3</code>.</li>
      <li><code class="code-chip">n</code> — optional number of clips, defaults to 1. Every video model returns one clip per request today (<code class="code-chip">max_n</code> 1), so a larger <code class="code-chip">n</code> answers <code class="code-chip">400</code> before anything is reserved.</li>
      <li><code class="code-chip">webhook_url</code> — optional; where to POST the completion event.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-4 text-fg-muted">
      Cost is the model's per-second rate × clip length × <code class="code-chip">n</code>,
      reserved up front and refunded if generation fails. Send <code class="code-chip">seconds</code>
      to fix both the length and the price. A model that returns fewer clips than
      <code class="code-chip">n</code> is charged for the clips it returned. To see the price
      first, send the same fields to
      <a href="#estimates" class="underline" style="color: var(--color-primary)"><code class="code-chip">POST /v1/estimates</code></a>.
    </p>

    <h3 id="video-inputs" class="scroll-mt-24 text-xl font-semibold mt-10 mb-4 text-fg">Start a video from an image</h3>
    <p class="text-fg-muted">
      A clip can start from an image, end on one, or follow reference images. The usual way to
      work: settle the look in stills, which cost cents, then animate the one you chose.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-fg-muted">
      <strong class="text-fg">Not switched on yet.</strong> These fields are part of
      <a href="#files" class="underline" style="color: var(--color-primary)">inputs</a>, which are
      being rolled out. Until they are switched on, a request that carries one answers
      <code class="code-chip">403</code> with <code class="code-chip">code: "inputs_disabled"</code>.
      A request without them is unaffected.
    </p>
    <pre class="code-block mt-4"><code>{videoInputSnippet}</code></pre>
    <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-fg-muted">
      <li><strong class="text-fg">First and last frame.</strong> <code class="code-chip">first_frame</code> is the image the clip starts from and <code class="code-chip">last_frame</code> the one it ends on. A last frame is taken only together with a first frame.</li>
      <li><strong class="text-fg">Reference images.</strong> <code class="code-chip">reference_images</code> is a list of images the clip follows: an object, a place, a look. A prompt refers to them by position, and the order of the list is the order they are counted in: on Seedance, "Image 1" (图片1) is the first in the list. Godance does not rewrite the prompt. On Seedance, reference images and a first or last frame are separate ways to work and cannot be sent together.</li>
      <li><strong class="text-fg">Animate one of your image outputs.</strong> Each of the three fields takes either <a href="#input-references" class="underline" style="color: var(--color-primary)">form of input</a>: a <code class="code-chip">file_id</code> for an upload, or a <code class="code-chip">job_output</code> for an image one of your own jobs made, which needs no upload.</li>
    </ul>
    <pre class="code-block mt-4"><code>{videoReferenceSnippet}</code></pre>
    <p class="mt-4 text-fg-muted">
      <strong class="text-fg">Which models take what.</strong> A model says what it takes under
      <code class="code-chip">godance.capabilities.inputs</code> on
      <a href="#models" class="underline" style="color: var(--color-primary)">GET /v1/models</a>.
      A field that is not listed is not taken.
    </p>
    <pre class="code-block mt-4"><code>{videoCapabilitiesSnippet}</code></pre>
    <div class="mt-4 card p-0 overflow-x-auto">
      <table class="w-full text-sm">
        <thead class="border-b border-border bg-bg-muted text-left text-fg-subtle">
          <tr>
            <th scope="col" class="px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold">Model</th>
            <th scope="col" class="px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold">First frame</th>
            <th scope="col" class="px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold">Last frame</th>
            <th scope="col" class="px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold">Reference images</th>
            <th scope="col" class="px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold">Image types</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-border text-fg-muted">
          <tr><th scope="row" class="px-4 py-2.5 text-left font-mono font-normal text-fg">seedance-2.0</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5">yes</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5">yes</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5">up to 9</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5">JPEG, PNG, WebP</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row" class="px-4 py-2.5 text-left font-mono font-normal text-fg">kling-3.0</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5">yes</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5">yes</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5">no</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5">JPEG, PNG, at most 10&nbsp;MB</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row" class="px-4 py-2.5 text-left font-mono font-normal text-fg">minimax-hailuo-2.3</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5">yes</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5">no</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5">no</td><td class="px-4 py-2.5">JPEG, PNG, WebP</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-fg-muted">
      <li><strong class="text-fg">Shape.</strong> An input image's width divided by its height must be between 0.4 and 2.5, and each side should be at least 300 pixels. Godance never crops an image. When a frame's shape is not the clip's, Seedance crops the frame from its centre; Kling takes the clip's shape from the frame.</li>
      <li><strong class="text-fg">People.</strong> Seedance does not take a reference image that shows a real person's face.</li>
      <li><strong class="text-fg">Price.</strong> The same as a clip from text. An input image is not charged for.</li>
      <li><strong class="text-fg">Refusals,</strong> each before anything is charged: <code class="code-chip">400 input_not_supported</code> (the model does not take the field, takes fewer reference images, or does not take the inputs together), <code class="code-chip">400 input_aspect_mismatch</code> (the image's shape is outside the range, which the answer names along with the image's own size), <code class="code-chip">415 input_type_unsupported</code> and <code class="code-chip">413 input_too_large</code> (the model's provider does not take that type or size), and everything under <a href="#input-refusals" class="underline" style="color: var(--color-primary)">what is refused, and why</a>.</li>
      <li><strong class="text-fg">The job</strong> lists what it started from, in the order given, under <a href="#job-inputs" class="underline" style="color: var(--color-primary)"><code class="code-chip">inputs</code></a>.</li>
    </ul>

    <!-- ===== Video (OpenAI shape) ===== -->
    <h2 id="videos-openai" class="scroll-mt-24 text-2xl font-bold mt-16 mb-4 text-fg">Video (OpenAI shape)</h2>
    <p class="text-fg-muted">
      <code class="code-chip">POST /v1/videos</code> and the routes beside it are OpenAI's Videos
      API over the same video jobs, so video code written for OpenAI's SDK works with the base
      URL, the key and the model changed. A video's <code class="code-chip">id</code> is its
      job's id, and the same job is at
      <a href="#jobs" class="underline" style="color: var(--color-primary)"><code class="code-chip">GET /v1/jobs/&#123;id&#125;</code></a>.
      <a href="#videos" class="underline" style="color: var(--color-primary)"><code class="code-chip">POST /v1/videos/generations</code></a>
      is unchanged; use whichever shape your code already speaks.
    </p>
    <pre class="code-block mt-4"><code>{openAiVideoSdkSnippet}</code></pre>
    <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-fg-muted">
      <li><strong class="text-fg">The model.</strong> <code class="code-chip">model</code> is a Godance video model, such as <code class="code-chip">seedance-2.0</code>, and it is required. OpenAI shut down its own Videos API and its <code class="code-chip">sora-2</code> models on 24 September 2026; its SDK still has these calls and marks them deprecated, and they work here.</li>
      <li><strong class="text-fg">Create.</strong> <code class="code-chip">POST /v1/videos</code> takes JSON or the multipart form OpenAI's SDK sends: <code class="code-chip">model</code>, <code class="code-chip">prompt</code>, <code class="code-chip">seconds</code> (a string such as <code class="code-chip">"8"</code>, as in OpenAI's API; a number is taken too), <code class="code-chip">size</code> and <code class="code-chip">input_reference</code>. It answers <code class="code-chip">200</code> with the video, <code class="code-chip">queued</code>. <code class="code-chip">webhook_url</code>{recipesOn && <Fragment>, <code class="code-chip">gallery_recipe</code></Fragment>} and <code class="code-chip">recipe</code> work as on <code class="code-chip">POST /v1/videos/generations</code>, and so do the price and its reservation.</li>
      <li><strong class="text-fg">Read.</strong> <code class="code-chip">GET /v1/videos/&#123;id&#125;</code> is one video. <code class="code-chip">GET /v1/videos</code> lists yours, newest first, as OpenAI pages: <code class="code-chip">data</code>, <code class="code-chip">first_id</code>, <code class="code-chip">last_id</code>, <code class="code-chip">has_more</code>; pass <code class="code-chip">last_id</code> as <code class="code-chip">after</code> for the next page (<code class="code-chip">limit</code> 1 to 100, default 20; <code class="code-chip">order</code> <code class="code-chip">desc</code> or <code class="code-chip">asc</code>). Every video job of yours is listed, whichever route made it.</li>
      <li><strong class="text-fg">Download.</strong> <code class="code-chip">GET /v1/videos/&#123;id&#125;/content</code> sends the file itself; it does not redirect. <code class="code-chip">variant=video</code> is the default. <code class="code-chip">variant=thumbnail</code> answers <code class="code-chip">404</code> with <code class="code-chip">code: "video_content_not_found"</code>: no model stores a poster image today.</li>
      <li><strong class="text-fg">Delete.</strong> <code class="code-chip">DELETE /v1/videos/&#123;id&#125;</code> deletes the video's files and prompt now, as the 90-day clean-up would, and answers <code class="code-chip">&#123;"id", "object": "video.deleted", "deleted": true&#125;</code>. Nothing is refunded. The job stays at <code class="code-chip">GET /v1/jobs/&#123;id&#125;</code>, <code class="code-chip">purged</code>, with what it cost. A video that is still queued or in progress answers <code class="code-chip">409</code> with <code class="code-chip">code: "video_not_deletable"</code>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-6 text-fg-muted">The video object. Times are seconds since the epoch:</p>
    <pre class="code-block mt-4"><code>{openAiVideoObjectSnippet}</code></pre>
    <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-fg-muted">
      <li><code class="code-chip">status</code> — <code class="code-chip">queued</code>, <code class="code-chip">in_progress</code>, <code class="code-chip">completed</code> or <code class="code-chip">failed</code>. <code class="code-chip">progress</code> is 0 until the video is completed, then 100. OpenAI's SDK polls every five seconds until the video is completed or failed.</li>
      <li><code class="code-chip">error</code> — set when <code class="code-chip">failed</code>: <code class="code-chip">&#123;"code": "generation_failed", "message": "…"&#125;</code>. The reserved credit is returned.</li>
      <li><code class="code-chip">cost_cents</code>, <code class="code-chip">job_url</code> — Godance additions. <code class="code-chip">cost_cents</code> is the amount reserved while the video is queued or in progress, the amount charged once it is completed, and 0 when it failed.</li>
      <li><code class="code-chip">expires_at</code> — when the files are deleted: 90 days after the video was created, or null while its job is <a href="#saving-a-job" class="underline" style="color: var(--color-primary)">saved</a>.</li>
      <li><code class="code-chip">size</code> — the size you asked for, or the model's default size where it has one.</li>
    </ul>
    <pre class="code-block mt-4"><code>{openAiVideoCurlSnippet}</code></pre>
    <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-fg-muted">
      <li><strong class="text-fg">Starting from an image.</strong> <code class="code-chip">input_reference</code> is the image the video starts from, the same as <a href="#video-inputs" class="underline" style="color: var(--color-primary)"><code class="code-chip">first_frame</code></a>. With OpenAI's SDK send a file: <code class="code-chip">input_reference=open("first.png", "rb")</code>; it is checked and stored as an upload to <a href="#files" class="underline" style="color: var(--color-primary)"><code class="code-chip">POST /v1/files</code></a> is. To use an image that is already stored, send <code class="code-chip">extra_body=&#123;"input_reference": &#123;"file_id": "…"&#125;&#125;</code> from the SDK, or in JSON <code class="code-chip">"input_reference": &#123;"file_id": "…"&#125;</code> or <code class="code-chip">&#123;"job_output": &#123;"job_id": "…", "index": 0&#125;&#125;</code>. An <code class="code-chip">image_url</code> answers <code class="code-chip">400</code> with <code class="code-chip">code: "image_url_not_supported"</code>. <strong class="text-fg">Not switched on yet:</strong> like every <a href="#files" class="underline" style="color: var(--color-primary)">input</a>, it answers <code class="code-chip">403</code> with <code class="code-chip">code: "inputs_disabled"</code> until inputs are switched on. A video from text is unaffected.</li>
      <li><strong class="text-fg">Errors</strong> are problem responses, as everywhere. On these routes each one also carries OpenAI's <code class="code-chip">error</code> object, so the SDK's exception shows the sentence and has <code class="code-chip">code</code> and <code class="code-chip">param</code> set.</li>
      <li><strong class="text-fg">Not supported,</strong> each with a <code class="code-chip">code</code> of its own: remix (<code class="code-chip">404 video_remix_not_supported</code>), edits (<code class="code-chip">404 video_edits_not_supported</code>), extensions (<code class="code-chip">404 video_extensions_not_supported</code>), characters (<code class="code-chip">404 video_characters_not_supported</code>) and <code class="code-chip">variant=spritesheet</code> (<code class="code-chip">400 variant_not_supported</code>).</li>
      <li><strong class="text-fg">Other refusals:</strong> <code class="code-chip">404 video_not_found</code> (also for a video that is not yours), <code class="code-chip">409 video_not_ready</code> (content of a video that is not finished), <code class="code-chip">400 invalid_parameter</code> (<code class="code-chip">param</code> names the field), and everything <code class="code-chip">POST /v1/videos/generations</code> answers.</li>
    </ul>
    <pre class="code-block mt-4"><code>{openAiVideoErrorSnippet}</code></pre>

    <!-- ===== Jobs ===== -->
    <h2 id="jobs" class="scroll-mt-24 text-2xl font-bold mt-16 mb-4 text-fg">GET /v1/jobs/&#123;id&#125;</h2>
    <p class="text-fg-muted">
      Returns one async job: its status, what was asked for and every file it produced. A job
      ID that isn't yours returns <code class="code-chip">404</code>. Fields are camelCase:
    </p>
    <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-fg-muted">
      <li><code class="code-chip">status</code> — <code class="code-chip">QUEUED</code> → <code class="code-chip">RUNNING</code> → <code class="code-chip">DONE</code> or <code class="code-chip">FAILED</code>.</li>
      <li><code class="code-chip">prompt</code>, <code class="code-chip">params</code> — the request, with its own field names: <code class="code-chip">size</code>, <code class="code-chip">n</code>, <code class="code-chip">style</code>, <code class="code-chip">quality</code> for images; <code class="code-chip">size</code>, <code class="code-chip">seconds</code>, <code class="code-chip">n</code> for video (<code class="code-chip">seconds</code> is the length generated, also when you left it out).</li>
      <li><code class="code-chip">outputs</code> — every file, in order: <code class="code-chip">index</code>, <code class="code-chip">url</code>, <code class="code-chip">contentType</code>, <code class="code-chip">sizeBytes</code>, <code class="code-chip">revisedPrompt</code> (null unless the model rewrote the prompt). Empty until the job is <code class="code-chip">DONE</code>. Each <code class="code-chip">url</code> is signed when you read the job and expires after a while, so read the job again for a fresh link rather than storing one.</li>
      <li><code class="code-chip">outputUrl</code> — the first output's link, signed the same way. Kept for clients written before <code class="code-chip">outputs</code>.</li>
      <li><code class="code-chip">mediaExpiresAt</code> — when the files and prompt are due to be deleted: 90 days after the job was created. Copy anything you want to keep before then, or <a href="#saving-a-job" class="underline" style="color: var(--color-primary)">save the job</a>. Null means nothing is scheduled, which is the case for a saved job.</li>
      <li><code class="code-chip">saved</code> — true when you have saved the job, which keeps its files and prompt past the 90 days.</li>
      <li><code class="code-chip">channel</code> — how the job was made: <code class="code-chip">API</code> with an API key, <code class="code-chip">STUDIO</code> from the web app. Godance sets it from the credentials the request used; no request field changes it. Null on a job made before this was recorded.</li>
      {recipesOn && (
        <li><code class="code-chip">galleryRecipe</code> — the slug of the gallery recipe the job was a run of, or null. To set it, send <code class="code-chip">gallery_recipe</code> in the body of <code class="code-chip">POST /v1/images/generations</code> or <code class="code-chip">POST /v1/videos/generations</code>: a recipe's slug, which is lowercase letters, digits and hyphens, at most 80 characters. Any other value returns <code class="code-chip">400</code> with <code class="code-chip">code: "invalid_gallery_recipe"</code> and nothing is charged. It is optional and changes nothing about the result or its price.</li>
      )}
      <li><code class="code-chip">recipe</code> — set when the job ran one of <a href="#recipes" class="underline" style="color: var(--color-primary)">your recipes</a>: the recipe's <code class="code-chip">id</code>, the <code class="code-chip">version</code> number it ran, and <code class="code-chip">variables</code>, the value each variable had. Null for a job made from a plain prompt, and again once that recipe is deleted.</li>
      <li><code class="code-chip">inputs</code> — the images the job was given, in the order the request gave them; empty for a job with none. See <a href="#job-inputs" class="underline" style="color: var(--color-primary)">a job's inputs</a>.</li>
      <li><code class="code-chip">purged</code> — true once the files and prompt have been deleted; <code class="code-chip">prompt</code> is then null, <code class="code-chip">params</code> is <code class="code-chip">&#123;&#125;</code> and <code class="code-chip">outputs</code> is empty. A <code class="code-chip">recipe</code>'s <code class="code-chip">variables</code> is null too: the values are deleted with the prompt. The cost fields and <code class="code-chip">channel</code> stay{recipesOn && <>, and so does <code class="code-chip">galleryRecipe</code></>}.</li>
      <li><code class="code-chip">errorMessage</code> — set when <code class="code-chip">FAILED</code>; the reserved credit is refunded.</li>
      <li><code class="code-chip">estimatedCostCents</code>, <code class="code-chip">actualCostCents</code> — integer cents: the amount reserved when the job was accepted, and the amount charged once it is <code class="code-chip">DONE</code>.</li>
      <li><code class="code-chip">id</code>, <code class="code-chip">modality</code>, <code class="code-chip">provider</code>, <code class="code-chip">model</code>, <code class="code-chip">createdAt</code>, <code class="code-chip">startedAt</code>, <code class="code-chip">completedAt</code> — timestamps are ISO 8601.</li>
    </ul>
    <pre class="code-block mt-4"><code>{jobSnippet}</code></pre>
    <p class="mt-4 text-fg-muted">
      <code class="code-chip">GET /v1/jobs</code> returns your jobs as a JSON array of the same
      objects, newest first. Without parameters it returns all of them. Optional query
      parameters:
    </p>
    <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-fg-muted">
      <li><code class="code-chip">limit</code> — 1 to 100 jobs per page.</li>
      <li><code class="code-chip">before</code> — a job <code class="code-chip">id</code>: only jobs older than it. Pass the last <code class="code-chip">id</code> of a page to get the next one; an empty array means there are no more. An <code class="code-chip">id</code> that isn't one of your jobs returns <code class="code-chip">400</code>.</li>
      <li><code class="code-chip">modality</code> — <code class="code-chip">IMAGE</code> or <code class="code-chip">VIDEO</code>.</li>
      <li><code class="code-chip">status</code> — <code class="code-chip">QUEUED</code>, <code class="code-chip">RUNNING</code>, <code class="code-chip">DONE</code> or <code class="code-chip">FAILED</code>.</li>
      <li><code class="code-chip">saved</code> — <code class="code-chip">true</code> for only the jobs you have saved, <code class="code-chip">false</code> for only the others. It combines with the parameters above.</li>
      <li><code class="code-chip">recipe</code> — a recipe <code class="code-chip">id</code>: only the jobs that ran a version of that recipe.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3 id="saving-a-job" class="scroll-mt-24 text-xl font-semibold mt-10 mb-4 text-fg">Saving a job</h3>
    <p class="text-fg-muted">
      A job's files and prompt are deleted 90 days after it was created. Save a job to keep
      them: a saved job is kept until you un-save it or close your account. Saving costs
      nothing and changes nothing about what the job was charged. Works with an API key or a
      signed-in session.
    </p>
    <pre class="code-block mt-4"><code>{saveSnippet}</code></pre>
    <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-fg-muted">
      <li><code class="code-chip">PUT</code> and <code class="code-chip">DELETE /v1/jobs/&#123;id&#125;/saved</code> return <code class="code-chip">204</code> with no body. Both are safe to repeat: saving a job that is already saved, or un-saving one that isn't, changes nothing. A job ID that isn't yours returns <code class="code-chip">404</code>.</li>
      <li>Only a <code class="code-chip">DONE</code> job whose files still exist can be saved. Any other job returns <code class="code-chip">409</code> with <code class="code-chip">code: "job_not_saveable"</code>.</li>
      <li>An account can save up to 500 jobs. Saving one more returns <code class="code-chip">409</code> with <code class="code-chip">code: "saved_limit_reached"</code>; un-save a job to make room. <code class="code-chip">GET /v1/jobs/saved</code> returns how many you have saved and your limit.</li>
      <li>While a job is saved, its <code class="code-chip">mediaExpiresAt</code> is null. Un-saving puts the 90 days back, counted from when the job was created. A job already older than that then has a <code class="code-chip">mediaExpiresAt</code> in the past, and its files and prompt are deleted at the next nightly clean-up.</li>
    </ul>

    <!-- ===== Files and inputs ===== -->
    <h2 id="files" class="scroll-mt-24 text-2xl font-bold mt-16 mb-4 text-fg">POST /v1/files</h2>
    <p class="text-fg-muted">
      Files and inputs. An input is an image you give a generation to work from: a product
      photo to place in a scene, or a picture to animate. Upload it once, then point
      requests at it. Works with an API key or a signed-in session.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-fg-muted">
      <strong class="text-fg">Not switched on yet.</strong> Inputs are being rolled out. Until
      they are switched on, uploading answers <code class="code-chip">403</code> with
      <code class="code-chip">code: "inputs_disabled"</code>, and so does any request that
      names an input. Nothing else about the API changes.
    </p>
    <pre class="code-block mt-4"><code>{fileUploadSnippet}</code></pre>
    <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-fg-muted">
      <li>Send <code class="code-chip">multipart/form-data</code> with the image in a part named <code class="code-chip">file</code>. <code class="code-chip">purpose</code> is <code class="code-chip">generation_input</code>, the only purpose there is; it may be left out.</li>
      <li><strong class="text-fg">What is accepted:</strong> a PNG, JPEG or WebP still image, at most 20&nbsp;MB and at most 40 megapixels. The type is read from the file itself, not from its name or the type the upload declares. An account can keep up to 200 files at once.</li>
      <li><strong class="text-fg">Metadata is removed</strong> before the file is stored: EXIF, XMP and anything else that describes the picture rather than being it, such as where and when a photo was taken. The image itself is not re-encoded. Its colour profile and its orientation are kept. <code class="code-chip">bytes</code> is the size of what was stored, so it can be smaller than what you sent.</li>
      <li><strong class="text-fg">Who can upload:</strong> an adult's account that has accepted the input terms. Accept them once in the app, under Settings; an API key cannot accept them for you.</li>
      <li><code class="code-chip">width</code> and <code class="code-chip">height</code> are in pixels, as the image is shown. <code class="code-chip">created_at</code> and <code class="code-chip">expires_at</code> are seconds since the epoch.</li>
      <li><code class="code-chip">contains_person</code> — what the automatic check found: <code class="code-chip">false</code>, or null when no check ran. While people are not accepted in inputs, an image that shows a person is refused, so a stored file never says <code class="code-chip">true</code>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3 id="input-references" class="scroll-mt-24 text-xl font-semibold mt-10 mb-4 text-fg">Pointing at an input</h3>
    <p class="text-fg-muted">
      Wherever a request takes an input, it takes one of two forms:
    </p>
    <pre class="code-block mt-4"><code>{inputRefSnippet}</code></pre>
    <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-fg-muted">
      <li>A <code class="code-chip">file_id</code> must be one of your own files. Someone else's answers <code class="code-chip">404</code> with <code class="code-chip">code: "file_not_found"</code>, exactly as a file that does not exist.</li>
      <li>A <code class="code-chip">job_output</code> must be an image from one of your own jobs that is <code class="code-chip">DONE</code> and whose files have not been deleted. Anything else answers <code class="code-chip">404</code> with <code class="code-chip">code: "job_output_not_found"</code>. It needs no upload.</li>
      <li>A reference that is not exactly one of the two answers <code class="code-chip">400</code> with <code class="code-chip">code: "invalid_input_reference"</code>.</li>
      <li>A request that is refused for its input charges nothing and creates no job.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3 id="file-retention" class="scroll-mt-24 text-xl font-semibold mt-10 mb-4 text-fg">How long a file is kept</h3>
    <ul class="space-y-2 text-fg-muted">
      <li>A file no job has used is deleted 24 hours after it was uploaded. <code class="code-chip">expires_at</code> says when.</li>
      <li>Once a job uses a file, it is kept for as long as that job is: 90 days, or until you un-save a <a href="#saving-a-job" class="underline" style="color: var(--color-primary)">saved job</a>. While a job uses it, <code class="code-chip">expires_at</code> is null.</li>
      <li>When the last job that used it is deleted, the file is unused again and gets another 24 hours.</li>
      <li><code class="code-chip">DELETE /v1/files/&#123;id&#125;</code> deletes it at once, whether or not a job used it. Closing your account deletes every file.</li>
      <li>A file is private. Only you can read it, through links that expire, and it is never shown publicly.</li>
    </ul>
    <pre class="code-block mt-4"><code>{fileRoutesSnippet}</code></pre>

    <h3 id="input-refusals" class="scroll-mt-24 text-xl font-semibold mt-10 mb-4 text-fg">What is refused, and why</h3>
    <p class="text-fg-muted">
      Each refusal is a problem response with its own <code class="code-chip">code</code>. An
      upload is checked in the order below, and the first check that fails answers. The size
      in bytes is checked before the type, and the size in pixels after it.
    </p>
    <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-fg-muted">
      <li><code class="code-chip">403 inputs_disabled</code> — inputs are not switched on.</li>
      <li><code class="code-chip">403 inputs_not_allowed_for_account</code> — the account is not an adult's.</li>
      <li><code class="code-chip">403 inputs_terms_required</code> — the account has not accepted the current input terms. <code class="code-chip">terms_version</code> in the response is the version to accept.</li>
      <li><code class="code-chip">413 input_too_large</code> — more than 20&nbsp;MB, or more than 40 megapixels.</li>
      <li><code class="code-chip">415 input_type_unsupported</code> — not a PNG, JPEG or WebP still image: another format, an animation, or a file that is cut short.</li>
      <li><code class="code-chip">409 input_limit_reached</code> — the account already keeps 200 files. Delete one to upload another.</li>
      <li><code class="code-chip">422 input_person_not_allowed</code> — the image shows a person, and images of people are not accepted yet.</li>
      <li><code class="code-chip">503 input_check_unavailable</code> — the image could not be checked, so it was not accepted. Try again later.</li>
      <li><code class="code-chip">429</code> — too many uploads in an hour. <code class="code-chip">Retry-After</code> says how long to wait.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3 id="job-inputs" class="scroll-mt-24 text-xl font-semibold mt-10 mb-4 text-fg">A job's inputs</h3>
    <p class="text-fg-muted">
      A <a href="#jobs" class="underline" style="color: var(--color-primary)">job</a> says what
      it was given in <code class="code-chip">inputs</code>, in the order the request gave them:
    </p>
    <pre class="code-block mt-4"><code>{jobInputsSnippet}</code></pre>
    <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-fg-muted">
      <li><code class="code-chip">role</code> — what the input was to the job: <code class="code-chip">image</code>, <code class="code-chip">mask</code>, <code class="code-chip">first_frame</code>, <code class="code-chip">last_frame</code> or <code class="code-chip">reference_image</code>.</li>
      <li><code class="code-chip">file_id</code> or <code class="code-chip">job_output</code> — the reference the request used.</li>
      <li><code class="code-chip">url</code> — a link to the input, signed when you read the job, which works for five minutes. Null once the file is gone: you deleted it, or the job whose output it was has had its files deleted.</li>
      <li>Once the job itself is <code class="code-chip">purged</code>, <code class="code-chip">inputs</code> is empty.</li>
    </ul>

    <!-- ===== Recipes ===== -->
    <h2 id="recipes" class="scroll-mt-24 text-2xl font-bold mt-16 mb-4 text-fg">CRUD /v1/recipes</h2>
    <p class="text-fg-muted">
      A recipe keeps a setup that works: a prompt template with variables, the model and the
      request parameters. Every change you save is a new version, numbered 1, 2, 3…, and a
      version never changes once it is written. Run a version with different values and
      Godance fills the template in, records which version and values each job used, and adds
      up what the attempts cost. Recipes are private to your account: an ID that isn't yours
      returns <code class="code-chip">404</code>. Works with an API key or a signed-in session.
      Field names are snake_case.
    </p>
    <pre class="code-block mt-4"><code>{recipeSnippet}</code></pre>
    <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-fg-muted">
      <li><code class="code-chip">POST /v1/recipes</code> — creates a recipe with its version 1 and returns it with <code class="code-chip">201</code>. <code class="code-chip">title</code> (up to 200 characters), <code class="code-chip">modality</code> (<code class="code-chip">IMAGE</code> or <code class="code-chip">VIDEO</code>), <code class="code-chip">template</code> and <code class="code-chip">model</code> are required; <code class="code-chip">variables</code> and <code class="code-chip">params</code> are optional.{recipesOn && <> <code class="code-chip">source_gallery_recipe</code> is optional too: the slug of the gallery recipe yours was made from.</>} A body that breaks a rule on this page returns <code class="code-chip">400</code> with <code class="code-chip">code: "invalid_recipe"</code> and a sentence saying which.</li>
      <li><code class="code-chip">GET /v1/recipes</code> — your recipes as a JSON array, newest first. <code class="code-chip">limit</code> is 1 to 100 per page, and <code class="code-chip">before</code> is a recipe <code class="code-chip">id</code>: only recipes older than it.</li>
      <li><code class="code-chip">GET /v1/recipes/&#123;id&#125;</code> — one recipe, with its latest version.</li>
      <li><code class="code-chip">PATCH /v1/recipes/&#123;id&#125;</code> — renames it: send <code class="code-chip">&#123; "title": "…" &#125;</code>. The title is the only thing about a recipe that changes in place.</li>
      <li><code class="code-chip">DELETE /v1/recipes/&#123;id&#125;</code> — deletes the recipe and its versions, with <code class="code-chip">204</code>. The jobs made with it are kept, and their <code class="code-chip">recipe</code> becomes null.</li>
      <li><code class="code-chip">GET /v1/recipes/&#123;id&#125;/versions</code> — every version, newest first.</li>
      <li><code class="code-chip">POST /v1/recipes/&#123;id&#125;/versions</code> — send <code class="code-chip">template</code>, <code class="code-chip">variables</code>, <code class="code-chip">model</code> and <code class="code-chip">params</code>. When any of them differs from the latest version, a new version is written and returned with <code class="code-chip">201</code>. When nothing differs, the latest version comes back with <code class="code-chip">200</code> and no version is written, so saving twice is safe.</li>
      <li><code class="code-chip">attempts</code> is how many jobs ran any version of the recipe. <code class="code-chip">spent_cents</code> is what the finished ones were charged, in integer cents.</li>
      <li>An account can keep up to 500 recipes, and a recipe up to 200 versions. Past either, the request returns <code class="code-chip">409</code> with <code class="code-chip">code: "recipe_limit_reached"</code>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3 id="recipe-templates" class="scroll-mt-24 text-xl font-semibold mt-10 mb-4 text-fg">Templates</h3>
    <ul class="space-y-2 text-fg-muted">
      <li>A variable is written <code class="code-chip">&#123;&#123;name&#125;&#125;</code>. A name is 1 to 40 ASCII letters, digits, spaces, hyphens or underscores, and starts and ends with a letter or digit.</li>
      <li>Each variable is declared in <code class="code-chip">variables</code> with its <code class="code-chip">name</code> and a <code class="code-chip">default</code>, which may be an empty string. Every declared variable has to appear in the template at least once.</li>
      <li>Every <code class="code-chip">&#123;&#123;</code> in the template has to begin a declared variable. A <code class="code-chip">&#125;&#125;</code> on its own is ordinary text, so a prompt written as JSON works.</li>
      <li>Filling in replaces each <code class="code-chip">&#123;&#123;name&#125;&#125;</code> with the value you send, or with the default when you send none. An empty string is a value. It is one pass: a value that itself contains <code class="code-chip">&#123;&#123;…&#125;&#125;</code> is inserted as it is.</li>
      <li>A template is at most 32,000 characters and has at most 20 variables. A default or a value is at most 2,000 characters.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3 id="running-a-recipe" class="scroll-mt-24 text-xl font-semibold mt-10 mb-4 text-fg">Running a recipe</h3>
    <p class="text-fg-muted">
      <code class="code-chip">POST /v1/images/generations</code> and
      <code class="code-chip">POST /v1/videos/generations</code> take
      <code class="code-chip">recipe</code> in place of <code class="code-chip">prompt</code>: a
      <code class="code-chip">version_id</code>, and <code class="code-chip">variables</code>
      with a value for any of the version's variables.
    </p>
    <pre class="code-block mt-4"><code>{recipeRunSnippet}</code></pre>
    <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-fg-muted">
      <li>Send <code class="code-chip">prompt</code> or <code class="code-chip">recipe</code>, never both: both returns <code class="code-chip">400</code> with <code class="code-chip">code: "prompt_and_recipe"</code>.</li>
      <li>The filled-in template is the job's prompt. It is what the model receives and what <code class="code-chip">prompt</code> shows on the job, and the request costs what the same prompt sent directly would cost.</li>
      <li><code class="code-chip">model</code> and the other request fields are sent as usual. The version's <code class="code-chip">model</code> and <code class="code-chip">params</code> record what worked; they do not limit what you send.</li>
      <li>A <code class="code-chip">version_id</code> that isn't one of your recipes' returns <code class="code-chip">404</code> with <code class="code-chip">code: "recipe_not_found"</code>. An image recipe sent to the video endpoint, or the other way round, returns <code class="code-chip">400</code> with <code class="code-chip">recipe_modality_mismatch</code>; a value for a variable the version doesn't declare returns <code class="code-chip">400</code> with <code class="code-chip">recipe_variable_unknown</code>; a value over 2,000 characters returns <code class="code-chip">400</code> with <code class="code-chip">recipe_variable_too_long</code>. Nothing is charged for a request that is refused.</li>
      <li>The job's <code class="code-chip">recipe</code> then names the recipe, the version and the values used, and <code class="code-chip">GET /v1/jobs?recipe=&#123;id&#125;</code> lists every job that ran the recipe.</li>
    </ul>

    <!-- ===== Estimates ===== -->
    <h2 id="estimates" class="scroll-mt-24 text-2xl font-bold mt-16 mb-4 text-fg">POST /v1/estimates</h2>
    <p class="text-fg-muted">
      The price of an image or video request before you send it. Nothing is charged and no job
      is created. Send the fields that set the price, with the same names as the generation
      request: <code class="code-chip">model</code> (required), <code class="code-chip">n</code>,
      <code class="code-chip">size</code> and, for video, <code class="code-chip">seconds</code>.
      <code class="code-chip">estimated_cost_cents</code> is exactly what the same generation
      request would reserve, with <code class="code-chip">n</code> and
      <code class="code-chip">seconds</code> filled in as the job would fill them
      (<code class="code-chip">seconds</code> is null for images). An unknown model returns
      <code class="code-chip">404</code>; a text model, or an <code class="code-chip">n</code>
      above the model's <code class="code-chip">max_n</code>, returns
      <code class="code-chip">400</code>. Works with an API key or a signed-in session.
    </p>
    <pre class="code-block mt-4"><code>{estimateSnippet}</code></pre>

    <!-- ===== Models ===== -->
    <h2 id="models" class="scroll-mt-24 text-2xl font-bold mt-16 mb-4 text-fg">GET /v1/models</h2>
    <p class="text-fg-muted">
      OpenAI's model list, public, no key needed. Each entry carries a
      <code class="code-chip">godance</code> object with its modality, list price
      (<code class="code-chip">pricing</code>) and platform fee
      (<code class="code-chip">markup_percent</code>). Image and video models may also carry:
    </p>
    <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-fg-muted">
      <li><code class="code-chip">display_name</code>, <code class="code-chip">description</code> — for showing the model to people.</li>
      <li><code class="code-chip">capabilities</code> — what the model accepts: <code class="code-chip">sizes</code>, <code class="code-chip">default_size</code>, <code class="code-chip">durations</code>, <code class="code-chip">default_seconds</code> (the length used when a video request leaves out <code class="code-chip">seconds</code>) and <code class="code-chip">max_n</code> (the most outputs one request can ask for). Each key is optional, and a missing one means not known, not unlimited. A model without <code class="code-chip">max_n</code> allows 1. <code class="code-chip">sizes</code> and <code class="code-chip">durations</code> are guidance; requests are not rejected on them.</li>
    </ul>
    <pre class="code-block mt-4"><code>{capabilitiesSnippet}</code></pre>

    <!-- ===== API keys ===== -->
    <h2 id="keys" class="scroll-mt-24 text-2xl font-bold mt-16 mb-4 text-fg">CRUD /v1/keys</h2>
    <p class="text-fg-muted">
      Send your key as <code class="code-chip">Authorization: Bearer gd_live_…</code>.
      A key calls the model API only: chat completions, image and video generations,
      estimates, jobs and usage. Everything else, these endpoints included, needs a signed-in
      session; a key gets <code class="code-chip">403</code> there.
      Create keys in the dashboard or with these endpoints:
    </p>
    <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-fg-muted">
      <li>
        <code class="code-chip">POST /v1/keys</code> with <code class="code-chip">name</code>
        (required) and optional <code class="code-chip">scopes</code> →
        <code class="code-chip">201</code> with <code class="code-chip">id</code>,
        <code class="code-chip">name</code>, <code class="code-chip">prefix</code>,
        <code class="code-chip">plaintext</code>, <code class="code-chip">createdAt</code>.
        <code class="code-chip">plaintext</code> is the key itself and is returned only this once.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code class="code-chip">GET /v1/keys</code> → your keys with
        <code class="code-chip">id</code>, <code class="code-chip">name</code>,
        <code class="code-chip">prefix</code>, <code class="code-chip">scopes</code>,
        <code class="code-chip">active</code>, <code class="code-chip">createdAt</code>,
        <code class="code-chip">lastUsedAt</code>, <code class="code-chip">expiresAt</code>.
        Never includes the key.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code class="code-chip">DELETE /v1/keys/&#123;id&#125;</code> → <code class="code-chip">204</code>;
        the key stops working immediately. Unknown IDs return <code class="code-chip">404</code>.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-4 text-fg-muted">
      An unknown, inactive or expired key returns <code class="code-chip">401</code>. A key
      restricted to an IP allowlist returns <code class="code-chip">403</code> from any other
      address.
    </p>

    <!-- ===== Billing ===== -->
    <h2 id="billing" class="scroll-mt-24 text-2xl font-bold mt-16 mb-4 text-fg">POST /v1/billing/topup</h2>
    <p class="text-fg-muted">
      Usage is paid from a prepaid credit balance. A top-up returns a hosted checkout URL;
      the credit lands on your balance once the payment provider confirms the payment.
    </p>
    <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-fg-muted">
      <li>
        <code class="code-chip">POST /v1/billing/topup</code> with
        <code class="code-chip">amountCents</code> and optional
        <code class="code-chip">save_card</code> → <code class="code-chip">201</code> with
        <code class="code-chip">sessionId</code>, <code class="code-chip">checkoutUrl</code>,
        <code class="code-chip">amountCents</code>. Send the user to
        <code class="code-chip">checkoutUrl</code> (Stripe Checkout).
        <code class="code-chip">save_card: true</code> keeps the card on file for auto-recharge.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code class="code-chip">POST /v1/billing/nowpayments/topup</code> with
        <code class="code-chip">amountCents</code> → same response; the checkout URL is a
        NOWPayments page for paying in USDT.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code class="code-chip">GET</code> / <code class="code-chip">PUT /v1/billing/auto-recharge</code>
        with <code class="code-chip">enabled</code>, <code class="code-chip">threshold_cents</code>,
        <code class="code-chip">topup_cents</code> — top up automatically when the balance falls
        below the threshold. Needs a card saved with <code class="code-chip">save_card</code>
        (<code class="code-chip">409</code> otherwise).
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-4 text-fg-muted">
      An amount outside the allowed range returns <code class="code-chip">400</code>; the
      error detail states the range.
    </p>

    <!-- ===== Webhooks ===== -->
    <h2 id="webhooks" class="scroll-mt-24 text-2xl font-bold mt-16 mb-4 text-fg">GET /v1/webhooks/secret</h2>
    <p class="text-fg-muted">
      Image and video jobs submitted with a <code class="code-chip">webhook_url</code> get a
      JSON POST to that URL when they complete. Each account has one signing secret:
    </p>
    <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-fg-muted">
      <li>
        <code class="code-chip">GET /v1/webhooks/secret</code> →
        <code class="code-chip">secret</code> (<code class="code-chip">whsec_…</code>) and
        <code class="code-chip">header</code> (<code class="code-chip">X-Godance-Signature</code>).
        The secret is created on first call.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code class="code-chip">POST /v1/webhooks/secret/rotate</code> → a new secret. For the
        next 24 hours deliveries are signed with both the new and the previous secret.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-4 text-fg-muted">The delivery body carries:</p>
    <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-fg-muted">
      <li><code class="code-chip">event</code> — <code class="code-chip">image.generation.completed</code> or <code class="code-chip">video.generation.completed</code>.</li>
      <li><code class="code-chip">jobId</code>, <code class="code-chip">costCents</code>, <code class="code-chip">createdAt</code> (Unix seconds).</li>
      <li><code class="code-chip">images</code> or <code class="code-chip">videos</code> — one object per output, each with a time-limited <code class="code-chip">url</code> (images also carry <code class="code-chip">revisedPrompt</code>).</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-4 text-fg-muted">
      Deliveries are sent once, on success, with a 10-second timeout and no retries. A failed
      job sends no webhook — if one doesn't arrive, poll
      <code class="code-chip">GET /v1/jobs/&#123;id&#125;</code>.
    </p>

    <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mt-10 mb-4 text-fg">Webhook signature verification</h3>
    <p class="text-fg-muted">
      Outbound webhooks carry an <code class="code-chip">X-Godance-Signature</code>
      header in Stripe format: <code class="code-chip">t=&lt;unix&gt;,v1=&lt;hex&gt;[,v1=&lt;hex&gt;...]</code>.
      Each <code class="code-chip">v1</code> is HMAC-SHA256 of
      <code class="code-chip">&lt;t&gt;.&lt;raw body&gt;</code>, so verify against the raw
      request body before parsing it. During the 24-hour grace window after a secret rotation, two
      <code class="code-chip">v1=</code> entries are included — verify against
      your stored secret and accept on ANY match.
    </p>
    <pre class="code-block mt-4"><code set:html={verifySnippet} /></pre>

    <!-- ===== OpenAI SDK compatibility ===== -->
    <h2 id="openai-compat" class="scroll-mt-24 text-2xl font-bold mt-16 mb-4 text-fg">OpenAI SDK compatibility</h2>
    <p class="text-fg-muted">
      Point the OpenAI SDK at <code class="code-chip">https://api.godance.ai/v1</code> and use
      a Godance key as the API key.
    </p>
    <ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-fg-muted">
      <li>
        <strong class="text-fg">Works unchanged:</strong> chat completions, streaming and
        non-streaming, and <code class="code-chip">GET /v1/models</code>. Catalog entries carry an
        extra <code class="code-chip">godance</code> object (modality, list price, platform fee
        and, for image and video models, what the model accepts) that OpenAI clients ignore.
        Video works through <code class="code-chip">client.videos</code>
        (<a href="#videos-openai" class="underline" style="color: var(--color-primary)">Video (OpenAI shape)</a>),
        and images through <code class="code-chip">client.images</code> with an API key set to
        <a href="#image-responses" class="underline" style="color: var(--color-primary)">synchronous image responses</a>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-fg">Same request, different response:</strong> image generation
        with a key that is not set to synchronous responses, and
        <code class="code-chip">POST /v1/videos/generations</code>, return
        <code class="code-chip">202</code> + <code class="code-chip">job_id</code>
        instead of the finished output, so the SDK's typed result won't contain images. Call
        these endpoints with a plain HTTP client (or your SDK's raw-response mode) and read
        <code class="code-chip">job_id</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong class="text-fg">Errors:</strong> non-2xx responses use RFC 7807
        <code class="code-chip">application/problem+json</code> bodies
        (<code class="code-chip">title</code>, <code class="code-chip">status</code>,
        <code class="code-chip">detail</code>) rather than OpenAI's error object. Status codes
        keep their usual meaning — <code class="code-chip">400</code> invalid request,
        <code class="code-chip">401</code> bad key, <code class="code-chip">402</code> insufficient
        credit or spending cap reached, <code class="code-chip">404</code> unknown model or job —
        so SDKs still raise their usual status errors. On the
        <a href="#videos-openai" class="underline" style="color: var(--color-primary)">OpenAI-shaped video routes</a>
        the body also carries OpenAI's <code class="code-chip">error</code> object, so the
        SDK's exception shows the message.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p class="mt-12 text-sm text-fg-subtle">
      Questions?
      <!--email_off--><a href="mailto:hello@godance.ai" class="underline" style="color: var(--color-primary)">Email us</a><!--/email_off-->.
    </p>
